      "description": "Dual Career (Spouse/Partner) Support - Human Resources | George Mason University Skip to content Quick Menu Home HR Directory HR Liaison Directory Forms HR News Quick Links Connect with Us Discounts | Patriot Perks Flexible Work Holiday Schedule HR Data Requests Income and Employment Verification Life Changes MasonLEAPS MyMasonHR Patriot Web Policies and Handbooks Contact Us Menu Careers Row 1 ⭐️ Job Openings ⭐️ Internal Job Board About Mason Accessibility Row 2 Dual Career (Spouse/Partner) Support Employee Type Definitions Employment Posters Equal Employment Opportunity and Affirmative Action Row 3 Military Members and Veterans Perks and Incentives Title IX New Employees Row 1 ⭐️ Onboarding Portal Campus Map Faculty and Staff Engagement Row 2 Faculty Retirement Options Military Members and Veterans New Instructional Faculty Support Row 3 Relocation Resources Supplemental Retirement Planning Well-Being Current Employees Row 1 Benefits Employee Assistance Programs Employee and Workforce Relations Flexible Work Row 2 Leave Life/Work Support Resources Performance Engagement Supervisory and Leadership Development Retirement Planning Row 3 Payroll Resources Reward and Recognition Professional Development Tuition Resources Well-Being HR Network Row 1 Classification and Compensation All-Inclusive Diversity, Access, and Engagement Recruitment Toolkit EPAF/Non-Benefited Onboarding Row 2 Exit Process Hiring Procedures Toolkit HR Liaison Reference Guides HR Technology Assistance Row 3 International Employment Personnel Action Policies and Handbooks Position Creation and Maintenance Supervisors Row 1 All-inclusive Diversity, Access and Engagement Faculty and Staff Engagement Hiring Procedures Toolkit Row 2 Onboarding Toolkit for Supervisors Performance Engagement Policies and Handbooks Row 3 Supervisory and Leadership Development Reward and Recognition Talent Acquisition Payroll Resources Retirement Row 1 Retirement Announcements Faculty Incentive Retirement Plan Faculty Retirement Options Row 2 Perks and Privileges Retiree Health Coverage Retirement Planning Row 3 Supplemental Retirement Planning Tenured Faculty Retirement Transition Program Virginia Retirement System (VRS) Menu Home Careers Job Openings About Mason Accessibility Employee Type Definitions Employment Posters Equal Employment Opportunity and Affirmative Action Military Members and Veterans Perks and Incentives Dual Career (Spouse/Partner) Support Title IX New Employees Onboarding Portal Campus Map Faculty and Staff Engagement Faculty Retirement Options Military Members and Veterans New Instructional Faculty Support Relocation Resources Supplemental Retirement Planning Well-Being Current Employees Benefits Employee Assistance Programs Employee and Workforce Relations Flexible Work Leave Life/Work Support Resources Performance Engagement Supervisory and Leadership Development Retirement Planning Payroll Resources Reward and Recognition Professional Development Tuition Resources Well-Being HR Network Classification and Compensation All-Inclusive Diversity, Access, and Engagement Recruitment Toolkit EPAF/Non-Benefited Onboarding eWork / PeopleAdmin / Search Resources (OLD) Exit Process Hiring Procedures Toolkit HR News HR Liaison Reference Guide International Employment Personnel Action Policies and Handbooks Position Creation and Maintenance Supervisors All-inclusive Diversity, Access and Engagement Faculty and Staff Engagement Hiring Procedures Toolkit Onboarding Toolkit for Supervisors Performance Engagement Policies and Handbooks Supervisory and Leadership Development Reward and Recognition Talent Acquisition Payroll Resources Retirement Retirement Announcements Faculty Incentive Retirement Plan Faculty Retirement Options Retiree Perks and Privileges Retiree Health Coverage Retirement Planning Supplemental Retirement Planning Tenured Faculty Retirement Transition Program Virginia Retirement System Forms HR News Directories HR Directory HR Liaison Directory Quick Links Connect with Us Discounts | Patriot Perks eWork / PeopleAdmin / Search Resources (OLD) Holiday Schedule HR Data Requests Income and Employment Verification Life Changes MasonLEAPS MyMasonHR Patriot Web Policies and Handbooks Contact Us Support Resources Dual Career (Spouse/Partner) Support Faculty and Staff Engagement Home Employee Relations Home Support Resources Home CommonHealth Crisis and Grief Disaster Assistance Employee Assistance Programs Faculty and Staff Success Networks Flexible Work Food Security Housing Assistance Parenting Employee Accommodations Relocation Resources Social Services Dual Career (Spouse/Partner) Support Tuition Resources Life Changes Updating Chosen Name and Pronouns Violence Prevention What is Dual Career Assistance? George Mason University’s (GMU) dual career assistance program provides career assistance to spouses/partners of George Mason faculty and classified staff. This program is offered by Human Resources to support recruitment and retention. George Mason employees may arrange for their spouse/partner to meet with a member of Talent Acquisition's Outreach and Compliance Team to discuss their career goals and role(s) desired, explore available opportunities, and review resumes and/or cover letters. In the event that the spouse/partner applies for an eligible position, the team will reach out to the hiring manager in that department to communicate that a dual career spouse/partner has applied.* Please note that this process does not automatically guarantee employment. *Spouses/partners must apply to a position via jobs.gmu.edu before the Outreach and Compliance Team makes contact with the department. What Do We Mean by \"Dual Career\"? Dual career is a term used to describe a scenario in which an employee is hired and their spouse/partner is interested in exploring opportunities for their own career development/employment. This can sometimes be a key factor in the recruitment or retention of an employee. George Mason University has put together resources and services to assist the spouses/partners of Mason employees. Dual career resources and services provided by George Mason University vary by department and are available for spouses/partners of faculty and classified staff. What Dual Career Resources and Services are Available at George Mason University ? George Mason provides consultative services to the employee and dual career spouse/partner. The employee and spouse/partner may arrange to meet with a member of the Outreach and Compliance Team to discuss strategies to finding employment. The team will review CVs/resumes and cover letters and provide guidance if requested. A Talent Acquisition consultant/recruiter may be brought in to assist in this review as well. There are no guarantees that this meeting will result in securing a position for the spouse/partner. Spouses/partners must apply to positions at jobs.gmu.edu before contact is made with the department. The Outreach and Compliance Team will reach out to the departments to communicate that a dual career spouse/partner has applied, should a position of interest be identified. During the Meeting Expand They will review the spouse/partner’s CV/resume, cover letter, and qualifications. They will then discuss the spouse/partner’s career interests, preferred jobs, what they do/do not want to do, likes/dislikes, what types of roles fit their skills and expertise, how far they want to travel to work, etc. The information will be used to explore strategies to find opportunities of employment. The Outreach and Compliance Team will search for possible opportunities on the jobs.gmu.edu site, and will share any identified jobs with the spouse/partner.  Spouses/partners are encouraged to search for and apply to jobs on both the George Mason University (GMU) and HERC websites; jobs.gmu.edu and HERCjobs.org, respectively as well. No CV/resumes will be circulated in lieu of an application being completed. A member of the Outreach and Compliance Team is available to meet with the spouse/partner for follow-up communication, or communicate via email throughout the search process. Services will be provided based on the spouse’s needs and engagement. Spouse/Partner Strategies for Finding Employment Expand We suggest the spouse/partner check out the George Mason University jobs site, jobs.gmu.edu site, and the HERC website, https://www.hercjobs.org/ for jobs that are available within the Washington, D.C., Maryland and Virginia area. It is always best to apply to jobs that match their skills, experience and desires.  We recommend setting up alerts so that when similar jobs come available, the spouse/partner will receive an alert and can apply immediately.  We suggest that when they find an opportunity of interest, they apply as soon as possible.  Don’t wait because studies show that candidates who apply earlier in the recruitment process typically receive more attention than those who apply later in the process. Recruiters have a lot to do in a very short period of time and spend on average seven (7) seconds per resume.  Additionally, we suggest that spouses/partners check with the DMV (Washington, D.C., Maryland and Virginia) Chambers of Commerce for businesses with which they may want to work, then search their job sites. Info for Prospective Mason Employees and Their Spouses/Partners Expand We provide dual career (spouse/partner) assistance/information to new employee spouses/partners.  Candidates who have not been offered a position and their spouses/partners will receive limited resources. Typically, what is offered at this stage are strategies to find employment, i.e. checking the jobs.gmu.edu site and the HERC website, https://www.hercjobs.org/ setting up alerts for positions that match their preferred skills, experience, and desires, and checking the DMV (Washington, D.C., Maryland and Virginia) Chambers of Commerce for businesses with which they may want to work, then search their job sites. The Outreach and Compliance Team is unable to circulate CVs/resumes for individuals whose spouses/partners have not received and accepted a formal offer from the University. If a candidate successfully goes through the recruitment process and receives an offer, or if they start their employment with GMU, then the new employee and/or spouse/partner can reach out to the Outreach and Compliance Team for a consultation. The Outreach and Compliance Team is unable to assist spouses/partners with developing interview skills but there are articles available on LinkedIn, Indeed, and Zip Recruiter that may assist the partner with honing their interview skills. Additional Resources Expand For a comprehensive list of resources at and outside of George Mason University, please review the Additional Resources PDF . Tips for Seeking a Job at George Mason University Submit prompt applications. Resumes, cover letters, and applications should be submitted prior to the full consideration date to ensure they will be reviewed. There is no guarantee that applications submitted after the full consideration date will be reviewed. Set up email alerts so you can be notified and apply early when positions that meet your desired qualifications become available. Make sure your resume and cover letter are up-to-date. Know your professional brand and display it in your resume and cover letter. Apply for positions even if you don't meet all of the desired qualifications. Underrepresented minorities and other disenfranchised groups often do not apply if they don't meet certain requirements, even if they would have been considered. Grow your network and let them know you are looking for work. Know how to speak to the requirements listed in the job posting and how you are able to meet them. Do your research on the companies you apply to and be prepared to speak about what you've learned. Be present in the interview with good eye contact, a firm handshake, confident posture, and a self-assured tone. Be flexible. Frequently Asked Questions Can you guarantee my spouse/partner a job? Expand No, but we will share their resume with other GMU departments and local institutions. Can a department create a position for my spouse/partner? Expand Very few departments have the ability/funding to create a personalized faculty role. The Outreach and Compliance Team recommends coordinating with them to contact the department on the spouse/partner’s behalf. Virginia Department of Human Resource Management (DHRM) policy restricts GMU departments from crafting personalized classified roles. Classified employees must complete a competitive search process before being hired into a position. Can a department pay for my spouse/partner to use Ayers Group services? Expand Certain GMU departments may choose to pay for and/or reimburse the cost of career consulting through HERC and the Ayers Group on a case-by-case basis. Most departments will not do so due to budgetary constraints. Can my spouse/partner get dual career assistance before I accept my offer of employment from George Mason? Expand In order for your spouse/partner to get dual career assistance, you must be a current employee or have accepted an offer from George Mason University and have an official start date. This does not preclude your spouse/partner from conducting their own job search and finding employment by their own means. Contact Us Ready to get started or just want more information? Fill out the Dual Career (Spouse/Partner) Intake Form or contact Talent Acquisition at jobs@gmu.edu . Find Us! Office Number: 4100 Alan and Sally Merten Hall GPS Address: 4441 George Mason Boulevard, Fairfax, Va. 22030 Mailing Address: 4400 University Drive, Mailstop 3C3 Contact Us! Email: hr@gmu.edu Phone: 703-993-2600 Fax: 703-993-2601 Connect With Us! Airplane icon that links to subscribe to HR & Payroll subscription page Subscribe to our newsletter Facebook Icon that Links to Mason's HR's Facebook Page /GeorgeMasonHR Twitter Icon that Links to Mason's HR's Twitter Page @GeorgeMasonHR Instagram Icon that Links to Mason's HR's Instagram Page @GeorgeMasonHR LinkedIn Icon that Links to Mason's HR LinkedIn Page /GeorgeMasonHR (opens in new tab) Mission Statement | Accessibility | Disclaimers Copyright © 2026 George Mason University Scroll To Top"
    },
